<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Prepositions</h1>
  <p class="topic-lead">Tips and traps</p>
  <div class="summary-stack">
    <section class="summary-block summary-peach">
      <h2>Position or movement?</h2>
      <p>
        The bird sat <strong>on</strong> the wall. It was already there.
      </p>
      <p>
        The bird flew <strong>onto</strong> the wall. It moved to that place.
      </p>
      <p>
        The toy is <strong>in</strong> the box. She jumped <strong>into</strong> the box.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="summary-block summary-green">
      <h2>Pairs that get mixed up</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Between</strong> is for two: between the two trees. <strong>Among</strong> is for a group:
        among the crowd.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Beside</strong> means next to. <strong>Besides</strong> means as well as: “Besides Maya, two
        friends came.”
      </p>
      <p>
        Useful pairs: <strong>good at</strong>, <strong>afraid of</strong>, <strong>interested in</strong>,
        <strong>listen to</strong>, <strong>arrive at</strong> the station, <strong>arrive in</strong> London.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="summary-block summary-lilac">
      <h2>The same word can change jobs</h2>
      <p>
        We ate <strong>before the show</strong>. <em>Before</em> is a preposition: a noun follows it.
      </p>
      <p>
        We ate <strong>before the show began</strong>. <em>Before</em> is a conjunction: a clause follows it.
      </p>
      <p>We waited <strong>before</strong>. Here <em>before</em> is an adverb.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
  <div class="trap-card trap-compact">
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>At, on and in</h2>
      <p>
        in Monday / at July / in 6 o’clock
        <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>on</strong> Monday, <strong>in</strong> July, <strong>at</strong> 6 o’clock
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        in the night
        <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span>
        <strong>at night</strong>, but <strong>in the morning</strong>
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>Use an object pronoun after a preposition</h2>
      <p>
        behind I / between you and I
        <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        behind <strong>me</strong> / between you and <strong>me</strong>
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
